some news from germany now and  chancellor olaf schultz has held his  first summer press conference continuing  the tradition of his predecessor angela  merkel  schultz answered questions on some of  the most pressing domestic and foreign  policy issues such as the war in ukraine  the rising cost of living and germany's  dependence on russia for gas that's  something the government is trying to  get on top of before the colder winter  months  dw also asked the chancellor about the  possibility of introducing a travel ban  for all russians schultz said he has a  very hard time with the idea and said  such a ban could undermine other  measures being taken against russia  we have discussed very extensive  sanctions which affect many specific  individuals and oligarchs and supporters  of russia's most powerful circles and we  will certainly continue to do so  it would rather weaken the effectiveness  of these measures if the winner directed  against everyone including completely  innocent people  so let's bring in our political  correspondent nina  nina to get us started why is this topic  on the agenda now  well our colleague asked this question  because schweitzer's counterparts in  estonia and in finland have raised this  topic they say it's not okay that  ordinary russians are still going on  holiday in europe in light of russia's  aggression against ukraine and olaf  schultz said a very clear answer to this  question he says sanctions must be  targeted and it will have to target the  powerful circles in moscow and there  cannot be a blanket ban on all russians  traveling to europe because that would  be contradictory the hope here in berlin  even if not voiced publicly is still  that the russian population will one day  direct their anger at the government in  moscow themselves but of course since  estonia and finland are eu countries  this is a topic that will be addressed  by the foreign ministers at their next  eu meetings later this month  now in germany in recent months the  government has repeatedly said that it  is fearing social unrest due to rising  energy costs and inflation did we hear  chancellor schultz replete repeat those  concerns today  no on the contrary but of course there  are estimates  that say that an ordinary german family  may have to pay an additional 1000 euros  a year for energy this year and this is  of course a lot there are lots of people  who get by on what they earn but they  don't necessarily have any savings and  so many people also from all of  charlotte's government have said that if  nothing is done then there could be  social unrest here once people are faced  with their energy bills so olaf charles  was sitting here trying to de outline in  detail again what the government is  actually doing to help people get  through this crisis and he did sound  very confident he said that the  government is doing two things use  maximum speed to win germany off russian  energy supplies but of course those  things take time which is why also the  government has already invested 30  billion euros in financial aid for  germans and that more money will come  tax relief will come also the social  welfare system will be reformed so that  people are not alone which is why  charlotte sounded so confident that  there will not be any social unrest this  year let's listen to what he had to say  no i do not believe that there will be  unrest in this country  in the way that has been outlined  and that is because germany is a welfare  state  former chancellor helmut schmidt once  described this as one of the greatest  achievements of german politics  and this welfare state must be effective  in this situation by clearly saying that  we will not leave anyone behind  i deliberately use the expression you  will not walk alone into the future  and that is also true that's what our  response to this is and that's why i'm  quite confident  well at the moment of course it's warm  in berlin so nobody needs to heat their  houses and whether or not charlotte's  prediction will hold true that there  will not be any social unrest we'll  probably only find out once people are  really faced with those higher energy  bills once it gets colder here  all right dw political correspondent  nina haza thanks so much for that update  you
 

